                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h1]DO NOT BUY THIS[/h1]\nWARNING: This game was released for Early Access over 5 years ago, and it was released in a VERY unfinished state, which is very sad because there's A LOT of potential here.\n\nTHE GOOD:\n- The concept is awesome. You play as someone deemed a criminal in a post-apocalyptical world, because she was sentenced by an elite society to work for them, or to be killed by these robots called Cleaners.\n- Puzzle battles. Devoid of weapons, you have to trick yellow and blue Cleaners to kill each other in order to advance, or use the environment to kill them. You also find and use special powers called Apps, which help you fight, but they never do damage directly.\n- It's a rogue-like, so when you die, you can come back to a \"different\" maze. You can level up your Apps by using them, and the Apps levels carry over between your runs, so your special powers are stronger each time.\n- Boosters help. They give you \"passive perks\" that you can upgrade and make your life easier as well, but progress doesn't carry between playthroughs.\n\nTHE BAD:\n- I managed to get through the 3 levels a couple of times and lost during the \"final boss\" fight both times (not sure if it's really the final, but it seemed like it). Each level has a preset group of rooms that can spawn, but the room pools are very small, so you'll likely see pretty much the same rooms EVERY SINGLE RUN. Beats the purpose of procedural generation.\n- The boss of each level is also always the same, so less replay value there.\n- Some NPC interactions felt too random, so you get screwed just because, and you can say goodbye to your run, pretty much like drawing a series of bad luck Chance Cards in Monopoly.\n- There's very little instruction/tutorial, so it took me a while to realize what I had to do, how, and why.\n- Achievements do not unlock properly. I should've gotten at least 5 more that I didn't, and got one that I shouldn't have.\n- The \"Unlocks\" section doesn't show your updated and unlocked Boosters.\n- Being a game that uses top-down 3D perspective, it's very brutal with jumps, necessary especially as a dodge measure.\n- Also related to height in this top-down 3D perspective, it's very frustrating that enemy projectiles and lasers feel like they always hit you, even when they shouldn't because of height.\n- When you load a game, your Apps show in the menu as if they're lv 1 with no XP\n- Useless menu. It has a whole section that is basically not used except for the start of the game (\"Messages\"), but seeing the basic stuff is a bit annoying, like food pills, and the menu often bugs out.\n- Unbalanced. There's this \"Hack Key\" (forgot the actual name) that is usually only once, in one room of the first level, but you can buy it over all three levels and it doesn't carry (only Apps' levels)\n\nTHE COMPLETE MESS:\n- Game crashes frequently, especially when you die.\n- Using teleporting Apps might make you get stuck or leave the the map area, forcing you to restart the whole level.\n- The game gets significantly harder, but it feels just chaotic, as if it morphed into a bullet-hell game, while you CANNOT shoot, and it ultimately stops being fun.\n- Terrible optimization. My Ryzen 5 2600 + RTX 2060 + 16 GB RAM can handle it pretty well with no FPS drops on max settings, but I had to turn shadows down to low because my computer was overheating, which it NEVER does, even on more complex games on ultra.\n\nVERDICT:\nWhile I think devs shouldn't have released this game in this sad state that it is now, I do hope they pursue this career. I didn't follow the development process, and I'd guess there's a reason why this went south. It's clear that they can create something really great, but sadly Subaeria, as it's being sold now, is NOT that 'something really great'... Far from it, just a wasted potential. Sad.",
